{
  "about": "Reference ranges used by inference/length_check.py to re-rank identifications when the user measures the bone. 'ranges' holds the greatest length of complete bones; 'widths' holds three widths that also work on broken bones: end_width (across an end, at its widest; either end, so a broken-off end counts), shaft_width (across the shaft at its narrowest) and head_diameter (the ball of the femur or humerus, or the hip socket). Keys are 'human' or one of the model's mammal family groups (config.MAMMAL_FAMILIES), then BONE_TYPES names; each entry says which animals it was measured on. A taxon/bone pair that is missing is simply not checked. Birds span sparrows to ostriches, so they get only an upper bound on length ('upper_bound_only'): the femur and tibia (tibiotarsus) of the ostrich, the largest living bird. That rules a bird out for longer bones and says nothing about shorter ones. Bird wing bones and bird widths are not covered.",
  "units": "cm",
  "measurement": {
    "long_bones": "Greatest length, end to end, of a complete femur, humerus, tibia, radius or ulna.",
    "flat_bones": "Greatest length, end to end, of a complete os coxa (hip bone) or scapula (shoulder blade).",
    "cranium": "Greatest cranial length, glabella to opisthocranion (front of the brow ridge to the back of the skull).",
    "end_width": "Greatest breadth across one end of a long bone (OsteoID's maximum proximal and distal widths, either end).",
    "shaft_width": "Breadth of the shaft at its narrowest (von den Driesch SD); for humans, the midshaft diameters front-to-back and side-to-side.",
    "head_diameter": "Diameter of the femoral or humeral head, or of the hip socket (acetabulum): von den Driesch DC (femur head depth) and LA (acetabulum length) for animals; Goldman FHD, HHD and AcH for humans."
  },
  "confidence_levels": {
    "high": "Measured specimen ranges agree with published population statistics.",
    "medium": "Measured specimen ranges from one reference collection; the true range may be somewhat wider.",
    "low": "Covers only part of what the class name includes (e.g. one wild species of a group with larger domestic forms)."
  },
  "sources": {
    "osteoid": "OsteoID Bone Identification, 'Max Length', 'Max Proximal Width' and 'Max Distal Width' on each species/bone page, https://boneidentification.com/bones/<species>-<bone>/ (accessed 2026-09-14 for the first lengths, 2026-10-02 for widths, os coxa, scapula and the added species). Ranges appear to include juvenile specimens.",
    "human_literature": "Adult population statistics used to check the human ranges: femur 39.2-49.0 cm (males) and 36.0-46.9 cm (females) in 200 Indian adults, https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8811676/ ; Southern Brazil means femur 46.4, humerus 33.5, radius 26.2, ulna 27.9, tibia 38.8 cm, https://www.bjfs.org/forensicjournal/download.php?arquivo=389 ; Spanish cadaver means, https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24611570/ ; female humerus 27.7 +/- 1.1 cm, https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7003718/ . Accessed 2026-09-14.",
    "craniometric_literature": "Greatest cranial length (g-op) averages about 18-19.5 cm in adult males and slightly less in females, with population means as low as about 16.2 cm reported; standard deviations about 0.6-1.0 cm. Accessed 2026-09-14.",
    "ostrich_morphometrics": "Kassem, Tahon, Khalil & El-Ayat 2023, \"Morphometric studies on the appendicular bony skeleton of the ostriches (Struthio camelus)\", BMC Veterinary Research, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10401756/ : 12 adult males, femur 30.00 +/- 0.23 cm, tibiotarsus 52.00 +/- 0.50 cm. Accessed 2026-10-01.",
    "goldman": "Goldman Osteometric Data Set (B. M. Auerbach): 1,538 adult skeletons from 59 locations worldwide, mostly preindustrial. Ranges are the 0.5th-99.5th percentiles of each measurement, left and right averaged. Measurement guide https://web.utk.edu/~auerbach/GoldMeasures.pdf ; data as packaged in https://github.com/geanes/bioanth (inst/extdata/Goldman.csv). Accessed 2026-10-02.",
    "open_context_zooarch": "EOL Computational Data Challenge: Zooarchaeology Metrics Dataset, Open Context, archived at https://zenodo.org/records/10742251 : 20,725 measured archaeological animal bones (mostly Neolithic to Iron Age sites in Turkey and the eastern Mediterranean) recorded after von den Driesch (1976). Values under half or over twice their genus's median were dropped as recording errors; ranges are the 1st-99th percentile from 100 bones up, else the full range, and need at least 10 bones. Ancient domestic animals were smaller than many modern breeds, hence the low confidence; only bovids (with cattle), pigs and the horse family are used, because the deer (fallow, roe and red deer) and dogs measured leave out elk, moose, wolves and big breeds. Accessed 2026-10-02."
  },
